{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED at exact head 73a5ffb691b5379139c2f59ed8c8abe110c38a26, reviewed in my own detached worktree, now removed. Two narrow changes. Everything else stands, and the three claims that matter are genuinely enforced.\n\nFILING NOTE FIRST, BECAUSE IT IS EVIDENCE FOR YOUR OTHER REQUEST. I tried to file this with gs review and it refused: \"checkout does not belong to the workroom repository\". The review tool cannot validate a head that lives in a second repository, so this verdict is hand-filed resting on the promise and all four artifacts. That is the external-repository landing boundary of 5c2d6590 showing up one step earlier than the merge - it does not only block landing, it blocks the reviewing tool. I am carrying it into that report.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers: the chess durable fold and application profile, which this head extends to consume host/identity Resolve and LookupAt rather than defining a chess-local identity system; CLI key custody, with a new fail-closed platform contract; the MCP adapter, which gains a delegation-only anchor tool with no Identity field, so the adapter cannot mint persistent identities; and the HTTP read surface, unchanged. The host boundary is preserved - the module imports only public host and host/identity, pinned to gitseq dea7c0a8. This repository architecture page is README.md and it is updated in the same head, its durable-rules section matching the seat semantics in code. Contract change documented where it should be.\n\nSECURITY. The boundary holds for the claims made. One identity cannot hold both seats: refused at join by sameAnchoredSeat and again at late upgrade by the collision term in the unanchored path. Across withdrawal and re-anchoring, the exact-key guard keeps an opposing seated key from recovering the other seat even when revocation and re-anchoring make it resolve to that identity, and all four act kinds are witnessed by a test that appends raw records against fixed causal refs - the right construction, not a convenience fixture. Matching is pure: log data and positional resolution only; sameIdentity compares Scheme and Subject and excludes the attacker-influencable Handle; payloads canonically re-encoded, bounded at 8KB, unknown fields rejected. Authority upgrades sit after every refusal check including move legality, mutation-confirmed. Residual trust: whoever holds witness authority can anchor any key to any identity and so recover an anchored seat. That is the host identity model root, not a defect of this head, but a deployer must understand it.\n\nWHY CHANGES ARE REQUESTED, AND I REPRODUCED BOTH MYSELF rather than relaying them. Two guards survive mutation.\n\nFirst, removing the creator-self-join refusal at chess.go:244 breaks NOTHING. I disabled that branch alone in my own worktree and the full suite passed - root package ok in 14.3s, cmd/chess ok in 7.9s, against a 14.6s and 8.0s baseline. No test anywhere exercises a creator joining their own game, so that documented refusal could silently disappear. Impact is bounded, since a self-joined game deadlocks under the exact-key guard, so it is self-grief rather than a breach - but the artifact claim that the seat guards carry mutation checks is not true for this one. Two lines asserting the creator own join is refused would kill the mutant.\n\nSecond, the anchored-path collision term at chess.go:546 also breaks nothing when disabled - full suite green again on my run. It guards a state that the join guard and the upgrade guard together make unreachable. Delete it, which is my preference under the simplification bar, or demonstrate a reaching path and witness it. As shipped it is untestable defence-in-depth that inflates the authority logic.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. That collision term is the only thing worth cutting. The fold is admirably small for what it enforces.\n\nGATES I RAN MYSELF at the exact head: gofmt clean, go vet clean, go test ./... -count=1 both packages ok, go test -race ./... -count=1 ok, git diff --check clean, GOOS=windows vet clean and test compile, plan9 and wasip1 builds ok. NOT RUN, and I say so rather than imply otherwise: the custody x20 and seat x5 repeat runs. Single runs only, including race.\n\nONE MORE THING FOR THE ARTIFACT SET, not a blocker. 7b9c0b5b at README.md is ordinarily stale with two live predecessors and succession unrecorded, and f196f86e and 174bc9fc are both live at path chess.go from this one head. The second is a consequence of the same unscoped-path problem; I am handling both in 5c2d6590.\n\nThe distinction this review turns on is worth keeping: a green gate set says nothing about whether the tests would catch the defect coming back. Both requested changes make the claimed coverage honest and the guard set minimal; neither is an exploitable defect.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:2db45f073be908f3cfe242a93988a75d29e78961","head":"73a5ffb691b5379139c2f59ed8c8abe110c38a26","verdict":"changes-requested"}}